window.STUDY_DAYS.push({"blocks":[{"label":"Warm-up","steps":["Re-read the Domain 2 task statements in the MLA-C02 exam guide (PDF) and highlight every mention of training infrastructure.","From memory, list the SageMaker instance families you know (m5, c5, p3, g5, inf1, trn1) and write one line on what each is for — then check yourself against today's notes."],"time":"0.5h"},{"label":"New material","steps":["Study the instance-selection notes below: CPU (ml.m5, ml.c5) vs GPU (ml.p3, ml.g5) vs AWS silicon (ml.trn1 Trainium for training, ml.inf1 Inferentia for inference). Rule of thumb: match the instance to the workload, don't oversize.","Learn managed spot training: `EnableManagedSpotTraining`, up to ~70% savings, interruptions are expected — so checkpointing (`checkpoint_s3_uri`) is mandatory, not optional.","Learn distributed training: data parallelism (SageMaker distributed data-parallel — full model copy per worker, sharded data, averaged gradients) vs model parallelism (model split across workers, for models too large for one GPU).","Read the bli55 notes on SageMaker training and the ccsusan SageMaker training notes; add instance/distributed/spot facts to your own notes."],"time":"2h"},{"code":"from sagemaker.xgboost import XGBoost\n\nxgb = XGBoost(\n    entry_point='train.py',\n    source_dir='src',\n    role=role,\n    instance_type='ml.m5.xlarge',   # CPU: right-sized for tabular XGBoost\n    instance_count=1,\n    framework_version='1.7-1',\n    enable_spot_training=True,      # managed spot: up to ~70% cheaper\n    checkpoint_s3_uri='s3://my-bucket/checkpoints/',  # REQUIRED with spot\n    max_run=3600,\n)\nxgb.fit({'training': 's3://my-bucket/data/train/'})","codeLang":"python","label":"Hands-on lab","steps":["In SageMaker Studio (or locally with the SageMaker SDK), write the training-job spec below: XGBoost on a CPU instance with managed spot training and an S3 checkpoint location.","Dry-run review: before launching, read the spec back and explain each parameter out loud — instance type choice, why spot is safe here, what the checkpoint URI does.","Launch it on a small dataset (or fully specify it on paper if you want zero compute cost). Watch the job in the console: note the instance provisioning, training, and checkpoint upload phases.","After the job completes, verify a checkpoint/model artifact landed in S3. Shut down the Studio kernel/app when done."],"time":"1.5h"},{"label":"Practice + teach-it-back","steps":["Do 20 practice questions on SageMaker training (ihatesea69 repo).","Teach-it-back, out loud, no notes: 'When would I choose ml.g5 over ml.p3, and when would I choose data parallelism over model parallelism?' Record where you stall."],"time":"1h"}],"day":11,"domain":"Domain 2: ML Model and FM Development (24%)","isCheckpoint":false,"isReview":false,"keyTerms":[{"def":"Cost-effective NVIDIA A10G GPU instance family for deep learning training and inference.","term":"ml.g5"},{"def":"AWS-designed chip family purpose-built for cost-efficient ML training.","term":"ml.trn1 (Trainium)"},{"def":"AWS-designed chip for inference only — never a correct choice for training.","term":"ml.inf1 (Inferentia)"},{"def":"SageMaker training on spare EC2 capacity at up to ~70% discount; interruptions expected.","term":"Managed spot training"},{"def":"S3 location where training checkpoints are saved so interrupted jobs can resume.","term":"checkpoint_s3_uri"},{"def":"Each worker holds a full model copy and trains on a different data shard; gradients are averaged.","term":"Data parallelism"},{"def":"The model itself is split across workers, for models too large for one GPU.","term":"Model parallelism"},{"def":"MaxRuntimeInSeconds cap on a training job — the cost circuit breaker.","term":"StoppingCondition"}],"notes":[{"heading":"Picking the right training instance","points":["`ml.m5` / `ml.c5`: CPU instances — preprocessing, classical ML (XGBoost, linear models), small jobs. Cheapest starting point.","`ml.p3`: NVIDIA V100 GPUs — heavy deep learning training. Powerful but expensive; don't default here.","`ml.g5`: NVIDIA A10G GPUs — the cost-effective GPU sweet spot for most deep learning training and inference.","`ml.trn1`: AWS Trainium chips — purpose-built for cost-efficient ML *training* on AWS silicon.","`ml.inf1`: AWS Inferentia — *inference only*. A classic exam trap: never pick it for a training job.","Exam rule: match the instance to the workload. Oversized instances are a cost-optimization miss, not a safety margin."]},{"heading":"Managed spot training","points":["`EnableManagedSpotTraining=True` uses spare EC2 capacity at up to ~70% discount for training jobs.","Spot instances can be interrupted with 2 minutes' notice — your job must tolerate that.","Therefore `checkpoint_s3_uri` is effectively mandatory: checkpoints save model state to S3 so training resumes instead of restarting.","Best for: long, fault-tolerant training jobs. Avoid for: time-critical jobs with hard deadlines.","Exam trap: 'spot training with no checkpointing' is always the wrong answer — interrupted progress is lost."]},{"heading":"Distributed training: data vs model parallel","points":["Data parallelism (SageMaker distributed data-parallel): every worker holds a *full copy* of the model, each trains on a *different shard* of data, gradients are averaged. The default choice — use it whenever the model fits on one GPU.","Model parallelism (SageMaker distributed model-parallel): the *model itself is split* across workers. For giant models (tens of billions of parameters) that don't fit on a single GPU.","Decision rule: model fits on one GPU → data parallel. Model doesn't fit → model parallel (sometimes both combined).","Multi-GPU single node is simpler than multi-node; communication overhead grows with node count."]},{"heading":"Anatomy of a training job","points":["Training image: a Docker image (ECR) with framework + your code — e.g., SageMaker's built-in XGBoost or a custom container.","Input channels: named S3 locations (`training`, `validation`, `test`) mounted or streamed into the job.","Objective metrics are extracted from training logs with a regex (`metric_definitions`) — the tuning/evaluation plumbing depends on this.","`StoppingCondition` (`MaxRuntimeInSeconds`) caps cost: a runaway job stops instead of billing forever.","Outputs: model artifacts and checkpoints land in S3 automatically — nothing persists on the instance."]},{"heading":"Cost hygiene for training","points":["Right-size the instance first — most wasted spend is an oversized GPU idling.","Prefer managed spot training for anything interruption-tolerant.","Always set `MaxRuntimeInSeconds` — it's your circuit breaker.","Training jobs terminate themselves, but failed/stuck jobs still bill while running — check the console after launching."]}],"objectives":["Pick the right SageMaker instance family for a training workload and justify the choice.","Configure managed spot training with checkpointing and explain the cost/interruption tradeoff.","Distinguish data parallelism from model parallelism and know when each applies."],"quiz":[{"answer":2,"options":["ml.m5.large","ml.c5.xlarge","ml.g5.xlarge","ml.inf1.xlarge"],"q":"You need cost-effective GPU training for a deep learning model. Which instance family fits best?","why":"ml.g5 (NVIDIA A10G) is the cost-effective GPU choice; m5/c5 are CPU-only and inf1 is inference-only."},{"answer":1,"options":["A VPC configuration","Checkpointing to S3 via checkpoint_s3_uri","An IAM role with AdministratorAccess","A larger EBS volume"],"q":"You enable managed spot training for a long job. What must you also configure?","why":"Spot instances can be interrupted, so checkpoints in S3 let training resume instead of restarting from scratch."},{"answer":1,"options":["Data parallelism","Model parallelism","Spot training","Serverless inference"],"q":"Your 40-billion-parameter model does not fit on a single GPU. Which distributed approach applies?","why":"Model parallelism splits the model itself across workers; data parallelism requires the full model on each worker."},{"answer":1,"options":["A shard of the model and all the data","A full copy of the model and a shard of the data","Half the model and half the data","Only the averaged gradients"],"q":"In data-parallel training, each worker holds:","why":"Data parallel = full model replica per worker, different data shard each, gradients averaged across workers."},{"answer":0,"options":["AWS-designed chips for cost-efficient ML training","GPU instances for graphics rendering","CPU instances for data preprocessing","Inference-only accelerators"],"q":"ml.trn1 (Trainium) instances are best described as:","why":"Trainium is AWS silicon built for training; Inferentia (inf1) is the inference-only counterpart."}],"resources":[{"label":"SageMaker developer docs — training","note":"Authoritative reference for training jobs, spot training, and distributed options.","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/"},{"label":"bli55 notes — SageMaker","note":"Community notes on SageMaker training specifics and instance selection.","url":"https://github.com/bli55/notes-aws-machine-learning"},{"label":"ccsusan notes — SageMaker training","note":"Exam-focused cheat-sheet style notes on training jobs and hyperparameters.","url":"https://github.com/ccsusan/aws-mla-certification-notes"},{"label":"ihatesea69 — hands-on labs","note":"Practice questions and lab walkthroughs for training topics.","url":"https://github.com/ihatesea69/aws-mla-study-notes-and-hands-on-labs"}],"title":"SageMaker training jobs","week":3},{"blocks":[{"label":"Warm-up","steps":["From memory: what is a hyperparameter (vs a model parameter)? Write two examples (e.g., learning rate, tree max_depth).","Recall yesterday's lab: which hyperparameters in that XGBoost job would you tune, and what ranges would you try?"],"time":"0.5h"},{"label":"New material","steps":["Learn Automatic Model Tuning (AMT): a tuning job launches many training jobs, each with different hyperparameter values sampled from ranges you define.","Learn the objective metric: you specify a metric name plus Maximize/Minimize; SageMaker extracts it from training logs via a regex (`metric_definitions`). If the metric never appears in the logs, tuning is blind.","Learn the three strategies: Bayesian (default — learns from completed runs to pick the next values; best when each run is expensive and you can afford few), Random (embarrassingly parallel, strong exploration baseline), Hyperband (adaptive — gives more resources to promising configs and kills poor ones early).","Learn early stopping (`early_stopping_type='Auto'`): underperforming training jobs are stopped before completion to save compute. Distinct from epoch-level early stopping inside a single training run.","Skim the SageMaker docs on AMT and the bli55/ccsusan tuning notes; note warm-start tuning (seeding a new tuning job from a previous one) as a bonus concept."],"time":"2h"},{"code":"from sagemaker.tuner import HyperparameterTuner, ContinuousParameter\n\ntuner = HyperparameterTuner(\n    estimator=xgb,                                   # Day 11 estimator\n    objective_metric_name='validation:rmse',\n    objective_type='Minimize',\n    hyperparameter_ranges={\n        'max_depth': ContinuousParameter(3, 10),\n        'eta': ContinuousParameter(0.05, 0.3),\n    },\n    metric_definitions=[\n        {'Name': 'validation:rmse', 'Regex': 'validation-rmse:([0-9.]+)'}\n    ],\n    max_jobs=20,\n    max_parallel_jobs=4,\n    strategy='Bayesian',\n    early_stopping_type='Auto',\n)\ntuner.fit({'training': train_s3, 'validation': val_s3})","codeLang":"python","label":"Hands-on lab","steps":["Write the HyperparameterTuner spec below for the XGBoost estimator from Day 11: tune max_depth and eta, minimize validation RMSE, Bayesian strategy, max 20 jobs with 4 in parallel, early stopping on.","Before running: predict out loud which strategy you'd pick if each training run took 3 hours and you had a $50 budget — and why.","Run it on a small dataset (or fully specify on paper if compute is a concern). Watch the console: jobs launch in parallel, poor ones get stopped early.","When done, inspect the best training job's hyperparameters. Then shut down Studio kernels/apps."],"time":"1.5h"},{"label":"Practice + teach-it-back","steps":["Do 20 practice questions on hyperparameter tuning (ihatesea69 repo).","Teach-it-back, out loud: 'My tuning budget is 15 training runs at 2 hours each. Which strategy and why? What would change if I could run 200 in parallel?'"],"time":"1h"}],"day":12,"domain":"Domain 2: ML Model and FM Development (24%)","isCheckpoint":false,"isReview":false,"keyTerms":[{"def":"SageMaker hyperparameter optimization: runs many training jobs over ranges you define to maximize/minimize an objective metric.","term":"Automatic Model Tuning (AMT)"},{"def":"Tuning strategy that learns from completed runs to choose the next hyperparameters; best for expensive, few runs.","term":"Bayesian search"},{"def":"Tuning strategy sampling hyperparameters uniformly; embarrassingly parallel, strong baseline.","term":"Random search"},{"def":"Adaptive tuning strategy that allocates more resources to promising configs and stops poor ones early.","term":"Hyperband"},{"def":"The named metric (Maximize/Minimize) extracted from training logs via regex that tuning optimizes.","term":"Objective metric"},{"def":"AMT terminating underperforming training jobs early to save compute.","term":"Early stopping (tuning)"},{"def":"Caps on total tuning cost and parallelism — the two budget knobs of a tuning job.","term":"max_jobs / max_parallel_jobs"}],"notes":[{"heading":"Automatic Model Tuning (AMT)","points":["A tuning job = an orchestrated set of training jobs, each with a different hyperparameter combination sampled from ranges you define (`ContinuousParameter`, `IntegerParameter`, `CategoricalParameter`).","You set the objective: metric name + `Maximize`/`Minimize`. SageMaker pulls the metric from training logs using your regex — no log line, no signal.","Limits matter: `max_jobs` caps total cost, `max_parallel_jobs` caps speed. Both are exam-relevant knobs.","The output is the best training job (best hyperparameters), which you then deploy."]},{"heading":"Search strategies","points":["`Bayesian` (default): builds a probabilistic model of the objective from completed runs and picks the next values intelligently. Best when each run is expensive and you can afford relatively few.","`Random`: samples uniformly. Embarrassingly parallel, surprisingly strong baseline, good when you can run many jobs at once.","`Hyperband`: adaptive resource allocation — starts many configs with small budgets, keeps the winners, kills the losers early. Best when you want to explore broadly without paying full price for bad configs.","Exam framing: expensive runs + small budget → Bayesian. Massive parallelism / broad exploration → Random or Hyperband."]},{"heading":"Early stopping in tuning","points":["`early_stopping_type='Auto'` lets AMT terminate training jobs that are clearly underperforming relative to completed jobs.","This is job-level early stopping (killing bad runs), not epoch-level early stopping (stopping training when validation loss plateaus) — the exam can test the distinction.","Saves the most money exactly when it's most needed: with bad hyperparameter draws you'd otherwise pay full price for."]},{"heading":"Reading tuning results","points":["The tuning job reports the best objective value and the hyperparameters that produced it — deploy that training job's model.","Hyperparameter importance / tuning visualizations in the console show which knobs actually moved the metric.","Warm-start tuning seeds a new tuning job with results from a previous one — useful when new data arrives and you re-tune.","AMT works with built-in algorithms, SageMaker frameworks, and custom containers, as long as metrics are logged."]},{"heading":"Exam traps","points":["If the objective metric regex doesn't match anything in the logs, tuning optimizes noise — always verify the metric is emitted.","Bayesian is not automatically best: with huge parallelism, random search explores more per wall-clock hour.","Spot training and AMT compose fine — each training job in the tuning job can itself use spot instances.","`max_parallel_jobs` too high with Bayesian wastes the strategy's intelligence (it learns from sequential results)."]}],"objectives":["Configure an Automatic Model Tuning (AMT) job: ranges, objective metric, strategy, and limits.","Choose between Bayesian, random, and Hyperband search strategies for a given scenario.","Explain how early stopping saves compute in tuning jobs."],"quiz":[{"answer":1,"options":["Random","Bayesian","Grid","Hyperband"],"q":"Which AMT strategy uses the results of completed training jobs to choose the next hyperparameter values?","why":"Bayesian optimization models the objective function from prior runs; random and Hyperband don't learn across runs this way."},{"answer":1,"options":["It runs every combination exhaustively","It gives more resources to promising configs and stops poor ones early","It picks hyperparameters randomly","It only works with XGBoost"],"q":"Why is the Hyperband strategy useful?","why":"Hyperband is adaptive resource allocation: winners get bigger budgets, losers are killed early."},{"answer":1,"options":["Stops training when validation loss plateaus within one run","Terminates underperforming training jobs before they complete","Prevents the tuning job from exceeding max_jobs","Stops the best job once it converges"],"q":"What does early_stopping_type='Auto' do in a tuning job?","why":"Job-level early stopping kills bad runs to save compute; epoch-level early stopping is a separate, in-training concept."},{"answer":1,"options":["Random","Bayesian","Hyperband with tiny budgets","Manual guessing"],"q":"Each training run takes 3 hours and you can afford about 20 runs. Best strategy?","why":"Expensive runs + small budget is exactly the Bayesian case: make every run count by learning from previous ones."},{"answer":0,"options":["You specify the metric name and Maximize/Minimize; it is extracted from training logs via regex","AMT always uses validation accuracy","It reads the confusion matrix automatically","It is inferred from the algorithm choice"],"q":"How does AMT know the value of the objective metric?","why":"You define objective_metric_name/type plus metric_definitions regex; if the metric isn't logged, tuning is blind."}],"resources":[{"label":"SageMaker docs — automatic model tuning","note":"Authoritative reference for AMT strategies, early stopping, and warm start.","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/"},{"label":"bli55 notes — SageMaker","note":"Community notes covering tuning strategies and exam angles.","url":"https://github.com/bli55/notes-aws-machine-learning"},{"label":"ccsusan notes — training & fine-tuning","note":"Cheat-sheet style summary of tuning concepts.","url":"https://github.com/ccsusan/aws-mla-certification-notes"},{"label":"ihatesea69 — practice questions","note":"Hands-on tuning questions and lab ideas.","url":"https://github.com/ihatesea69/aws-mla-study-notes-and-hands-on-labs"}],"title":"Hyperparameter tuning","week":3},{"blocks":[{"label":"Warm-up","steps":["From memory, write the formulas for precision and recall. Then write one sentence on when you'd optimize each.","Skim your Day 1 notes on overfitting — today you learn the metrics that detect it and the techniques that fight it."],"time":"0.5h"},{"label":"New material","steps":["Master the confusion matrix: TP, FP, TN, FN. Derive precision = TP/(TP+FP), recall = TP/(TP+FN), F1 = harmonic mean of the two. Know why accuracy lies on imbalanced data (99% accuracy can mean 'always predicts the majority class').","Learn AUC-ROC: ranking quality across all thresholds; 0.5 = random, 1.0 = perfect. Know when it's preferred over a single-threshold metric.","Learn regression metrics: RMSE (square root of mean squared error — punishes large errors), MAE (mean absolute error — robust to outliers), R² (variance explained).","Learn the selection rule: match the metric to the cost of being wrong. Fraud/medical screening → recall (missing a positive is expensive). Spam filtering → precision (false alarms are expensive).","Learn regularization: L1/Lasso (drives weights to exactly zero → sparsity/feature selection), L2/Ridge (shrinks weights smoothly), dropout (randomly zeroes neurons during training), early stopping.","Learn Bedrock model evaluation: automatic evaluation (built-in prompt datasets, metrics like ROUGE/BERTScore for summarization/Q&A/classification) vs human evaluation; LLM quality dimensions — correctness, relevance, groundedness/faithfulness, toxicity, fluency."],"time":"2h"},{"code":"from sklearn.metrics import precision_score, recall_score, f1_score, confusion_matrix\n\ny_true = [1, 0, 1, 1, 0, 1, 0, 0]\ny_pred = [1, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1, 1, 0]\n\nprint('precision:', round(precision_score(y_true, y_pred), 3))  # TP / (TP + FP)\nprint('recall:   ', round(recall_score(y_true, y_pred), 3))     # TP / (TP + FN)\nprint('F1:       ', round(f1_score(y_true, y_pred), 3))\nprint(confusion_matrix(y_true, y_pred))  # rows = actual, cols = predicted","codeLang":"python","label":"Hands-on lab","steps":["Run the snippet below: compute precision, recall, F1, and the confusion matrix on a small binary example by hand first, then verify with sklearn.","Modify y_pred to predict all zeros — watch accuracy stay high-ish while recall collapses to 0. That's the 'accuracy lies' demo; write one sentence on what it proves.","On the Iris data from Day 1, train two classifiers and compare them with F1 and ROC-AUC instead of accuracy. Note where the ranking differs.","In the Bedrock console (or on paper), sketch an evaluation plan for a summarization model: which automatic metrics, which human dimensions, and what dataset you'd use."],"time":"1.5h"},{"label":"Practice + teach-it-back","steps":["Do 20 practice questions on metrics and regularization (ihatesea69 repo).","Teach-it-back, out loud: 'My fraud model has 99.2% accuracy and I'm still unhappy. Walk me through why, using the confusion matrix, and tell me which metric I'd track instead.'"],"time":"1h"}],"day":13,"domain":"Domain 2: ML Model and FM Development (24%)","isCheckpoint":false,"isReview":false,"keyTerms":[{"def":"TP/(TP+FP): of all positive predictions, the fraction actually correct.","term":"Precision"},{"def":"TP/(TP+FN): of all actual positives, the fraction the model caught.","term":"Recall"},{"def":"Harmonic mean of precision and recall; punishes one-sided models.","term":"F1"},{"def":"Area under the ROC curve; threshold-independent ranking quality, 0.5 = random.","term":"AUC-ROC"},{"def":"Regression error metrics; RMSE punishes large errors more, MAE is outlier-robust.","term":"RMSE / MAE"},{"def":"L1 (Lasso) sparsifies weights to zero; L2 (Ridge) shrinks them smoothly.","term":"L1 / L2 regularization"},{"def":"Randomly zeroing neurons during training to reduce overfitting in neural nets.","term":"Dropout"},{"def":"Whether an LLM's answer is supported by its source context — the key RAG quality dimension.","term":"Groundedness / faithfulness"}],"notes":[{"heading":"The confusion matrix","points":["Rows = actual class, columns = predicted class. TP: predicted positive, actually positive. FP: predicted positive, actually negative (false alarm). FN: predicted negative, actually positive (miss). TN: correctly negative.","`precision = TP / (TP + FP)`: of everything flagged positive, how much was truly positive.","`recall = TP / (TP + FN)`: of all actual positives, how many did we catch.","`F1 = 2·P·R / (P + R)`: harmonic mean — punishes a model that's good at only one of the two.","Accuracy = (TP + TN) / total. On 99-to-1 imbalanced data, 'always predict negative' scores 99% accuracy and is useless."]},{"heading":"AUC-ROC and regression metrics","points":["ROC curve plots true-positive rate vs false-positive rate across all thresholds; `AUC` summarizes it. 0.5 = random, 1.0 = perfect ranking.","Use AUC-ROC when you care about ranking quality independent of any single threshold — common in exam scenarios.","`RMSE`: square root of mean squared error — large errors are squared, so outliers dominate. Use when big misses are very costly.","`MAE`: mean absolute error — treats all errors linearly, robust to outliers.","`R²`: fraction of variance explained; 1.0 is perfect, 0 means 'as good as predicting the mean'."]},{"heading":"Choosing the metric","points":["Match the metric to the business cost of each error type — the exam loves this framing.","Missing a positive is expensive (fraud, disease screening) → optimize `recall`.","False alarms are expensive (spam filtering, expensive manual review) → optimize `precision`.","Need balance or comparing models on skewed data → `F1` or `AUC-ROC`, never raw accuracy.","Regression with costly large errors (pricing, forecasting) → `RMSE`; with noisy outliers → `MAE`."]},{"heading":"Regularization","points":["`L1 (Lasso)`: penalty on absolute weights — drives some weights to exactly zero, producing sparse models and implicit feature selection.","`L2 (Ridge)`: penalty on squared weights — shrinks all weights smoothly toward zero without eliminating them.","`Dropout`: during training, randomly zeroes a fraction of neurons each step — prevents co-adaptation, standard in neural nets.","Early stopping: halt training when validation loss stops improving — free regularization, no extra hyperparameter to tune.","All of these trade a little bias for a lot less variance — the Day 1 tradeoff made concrete."]},{"heading":"LLM evaluation on Bedrock","points":["Bedrock model evaluation jobs come in two flavors: `automatic` (built-in prompt datasets + computed metrics) and `human` (reviewers rating outputs).","Automatic metrics depend on task: summarization/Q&A/classification/text generation each get relevant built-in metric sets (e.g., ROUGE, BERTScore-style similarity).","Human evaluation rates dimensions like correctness, relevance, and groundedness/faithfulness (is the answer supported by the source?).","Know the split: automatic = fast, cheap, repeatable; human = nuanced judgment (toxicity, helpfulness) that metrics can't capture.","Exam angle: 'evaluate RAG answer quality' → groundedness/faithfulness against retrieved context is the key dimension."]}],"objectives":["Choose the right metric (precision, recall, F1, AUC-ROC, RMSE, MAE) for a given problem and explain why accuracy can mislead.","Read a confusion matrix and compute precision/recall from it.","Contrast L1 vs L2 regularization and describe how Bedrock evaluates LLM outputs."],"quiz":[{"answer":0,"options":["90%","Cannot tell from this info","10%","100%"],"q":"A spam filter flags 100 emails as spam; 90 truly are spam. What is its precision?","why":"Precision = TP/(TP+FP) = 90/100 = 90%."},{"answer":1,"options":["The model is excellent","Accuracy misleads on imbalanced data — low recall means most fraud is missed","The model is overfit, proven by these numbers","More features will fix the accuracy"],"q":"A fraud model has 99% accuracy but 40% recall. What's the right conclusion?","why":"High accuracy with low recall on skewed data means the model mostly predicts the majority class and misses the positives you care about."},{"answer":2,"options":["L2 (Ridge)","Dropout","L1 (Lasso)","Early stopping"],"q":"Which regularization technique drives some feature weights to exactly zero?","why":"L1's absolute-value penalty sparsifies weights to zero (feature selection); L2 only shrinks them."},{"answer":2,"options":["MAE","R²","RMSE","MAPE"],"q":"A few very large errors would be catastrophic for your use case. Which regression metric punishes them most?","why":"RMSE squares errors before averaging, so large errors dominate the score."},{"answer":1,"options":["Fluency","Groundedness / faithfulness","Latency","Throughput"],"q":"You're evaluating a RAG system. Which dimension checks that answers stay supported by the retrieved documents?","why":"Groundedness (faithfulness) measures whether the output is supported by the source context — the core RAG quality check."}],"resources":[{"label":"MLA-C02 exam guide (PDF)","note":"Check which evaluation concepts appear in the Domain 2 task statements.","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/pdfs/aws-certification/latest/machine-learning-engineer-associate-02/machine-learning-engineer-associate-02.pdf"},{"label":"bli55 notes — ML fundamentals","note":"Metrics, confusion matrix, and regularization notes.","url":"https://github.com/bli55/notes-aws-machine-learning"},{"label":"ccsusan notes — model training & evaluation","note":"Exam-focused metric definitions and comparison tables.","url":"https://github.com/ccsusan/aws-mla-certification-notes"},{"label":"Skill Builder — MLA-C02 prep","note":"Official prep; look for Bedrock model evaluation coverage.","url":"https://skillbuilder.aws"}],"title":"Evaluation metrics & regularization","week":3},{"blocks":[{"label":"Warm-up","steps":["From memory: what is a foundation model, and why don't teams train them from scratch? (One paragraph, no notes.)","Skim the Domain 2 task statements in the exam guide and mark every mention of foundation models, Bedrock, or generative AI — this is the highest-ROI new content on the exam."],"time":"0.5h"},{"label":"New material","steps":["Learn Bedrock basics: serverless access to foundation models (Amazon Nova, Anthropic Claude, Meta Llama, Mistral, and others) through one AWS service — no infrastructure to manage. Note inference parameters: temperature, top_p, max tokens.","Learn the Converse API: a single unified chat API (`converse`) across all Bedrock models — messages with roles, system prompts, and tool use — replacing per-model InvokeModel payload formats. Know when the exam expects 'Converse API' as the answer (model-agnostic chat).","Learn the three Bedrock customization paths and their data: fine-tuning (labeled task data → adapts model to a specific task), continued pre-training (unlabeled domain corpus → teaches domain language/knowledge), distillation (large teacher's outputs train a smaller, cheaper, faster student).","Learn Guardrails: input/output policies independent of the model — content filters, denied topics, word filters, sensitive-information (PII) filters, and contextual grounding checks.","Learn Knowledge Bases: managed RAG — point at S3 data, Bedrock handles chunking/embeddings/vector store; query via Retrieve or RetrieveAndGenerate APIs with zero retrieval code.","Read the bli55 Bedrock/agentic notes for the current service details; use the exam guide as the scope authority."],"time":"2h"},{"code":"import boto3\n\nbedrock = boto3.client('bedrock-runtime', region_name='us-east-1')\nresp = bedrock.converse(\n    modelId='amazon.nova-micro-v1:0',\n    messages=[{'role': 'user', 'content': [{'text': 'What is overfitting?'}]}],\n    inferenceConfig={'maxTokens': 200, 'temperature': 0.3},\n)\nprint(resp['output']['message']['content'][0]['text'])","codeLang":"python","label":"Hands-on lab","steps":["Open the Bedrock console → chat playground. Run the snippet below (or use the playground UI) to call a model via the Converse API. Vary temperature (0 vs 1) and observe the output difference.","In the console, create a Knowledge Base: upload 3–5 text documents to S3, let Bedrock chunk and embed them into the default vector store, then ask a question answerable only from your documents via RetrieveAndGenerate.","Verify the answer cites your documents — that's groundedness working. Then delete the Knowledge Base and any provisioned resources. Bedrock on-demand inference is pay-per-use; custom models/provisioned throughput bill hourly — don't leave them running."],"time":"1.5h"},{"label":"Practice + teach-it-back","steps":["Do 20 practice questions on Bedrock/generative AI (Tutorials Dojo MLA-C02 set or Skill Builder).","Teach-it-back, out loud: 'I have 10 GB of unlabeled legal contracts and 500 labeled Q&A pairs. I want a fast, cheap model for contract Q&A. Walk through my Bedrock options in order.'"],"time":"1h"}],"day":14,"domain":"Domain 2: ML Model and FM Development (24%)","isCheckpoint":false,"isReview":false,"keyTerms":[{"def":"Serverless AWS service providing API access to foundation models without managing infrastructure.","term":"Amazon Bedrock"},{"def":"Unified Bedrock chat API (messages, system prompts, tool use) that works across FM providers.","term":"Converse API"},{"def":"Supervised adaptation of an FM on labeled task data.","term":"Fine-tuning"},{"def":"Adapting an FM on unlabeled domain text to teach it domain language/knowledge.","term":"Continued pre-training"},{"def":"Training a small, cheap student model on a large teacher model's outputs.","term":"Distillation"},{"def":"Bedrock input/output policy layer: content filters, denied topics, PII filters, grounding checks.","term":"Guardrails"},{"def":"Bedrock managed RAG: S3 data → embeddings → vector store, queried via Retrieve/RetrieveAndGenerate.","term":"Knowledge Bases"},{"def":"Reserved Bedrock model capacity billed hourly; for consistent high-volume or custom-model workloads.","term":"Provisioned throughput"}],"notes":[{"heading":"Foundation models on Bedrock","points":["Bedrock = serverless API access to FMs (Amazon Nova, Anthropic Claude, Meta Llama, Mistral, others). No GPUs to provision, no serving stack to run.","Key inference parameters: `temperature` (randomness), `top_p` (nucleus sampling), `maxTokens` (output length cap). Low temperature → deterministic; high → creative.","Model access must be granted in the console (use-case details) before calling a model — an exam-relevant operational step.","On-demand (pay per token) vs provisioned throughput (reserved capacity, hourly billing) for consistent high-volume workloads."]},{"heading":"The Converse API","points":["`converse` is the unified chat API across Bedrock models: `messages` with `role`/`content`, `system` prompts, and `inferenceConfig` in one standard shape.","It replaces juggling per-model `InvokeModel` payload formats (each provider's JSON was different) — one code path for many models.","Supports tool use (function calling): the model can request tool calls and you return results in the conversation loop.","Exam signal: any scenario asking for model-agnostic chat across providers → Converse API."]},{"heading":"Customizing models: fine-tune vs continued pre-train vs distill","points":["`Fine-tuning`: supervised, on *labeled* task data (prompt → desired response pairs). Best when you want the model to perform a specific task your way.","`Continued pre-training`: on *unlabeled* domain corpus (journals, contracts, manuals). Teaches the model domain language and knowledge before any task tuning.","`Distillation`: a large teacher model's outputs become training data for a smaller student — keeps most quality at a fraction of the cost/latency.","Decision order: need domain knowledge → continued pre-training; need task behavior → fine-tuning; need it cheap and fast → distill. They compose: pre-train, then fine-tune, then distill.","Custom models on Bedrock are deployed with provisioned throughput — hourly billing, so this is a cost-hygiene exam topic too."]},{"heading":"Guardrails","points":["Guardrails = policy layer applied to inputs and outputs, independent of the model: content filters (hate, violence, sexual, misconduct), denied topics, word filters, sensitive-information filters (PII redaction).","Contextual grounding check: verifies responses are grounded in the provided source — the managed answer to RAG hallucination.","Applied per-application via a guardrail ID/version — same model, different policies per use case.","Exam trap: Guardrails filter and block; they do not train, fine-tune, or improve the model."]},{"heading":"Knowledge Bases (managed RAG)","points":["Point a Knowledge Base at S3 data; Bedrock handles chunking, embeddings, and the vector store (e.g., OpenSearch Serverless) automatically.","Query with `Retrieve` (get chunks) or `RetrieveAndGenerate` (chunks + FM answer in one call) — no retrieval code to write or operate.","Data stays in your account; the FM only sees retrieved chunks at query time — the standard enterprise answer to 'use our private data with an FM'.","Sync the data source when documents change; stale index = stale answers."]},{"heading":"Bedrock model evaluation","points":["Evaluation jobs: `automatic` (built-in datasets, computed metrics per task type) vs `human` (reviewer workflows).","Use it to compare base vs fine-tuned vs distilled models on *your* prompts before committing to provisioned throughput.","Bring-your-own prompt datasets make the evaluation reflect your actual use case, not a generic benchmark."]}],"objectives":["Use the Converse API to interact with Bedrock foundation models and explain why it's the unified choice.","Distinguish fine-tuning, continued pre-training, and distillation on Bedrock — and pick the right one for a scenario.","Describe Guardrails and Knowledge Bases and what each adds to a generative AI application."],"quiz":[{"answer":1,"options":["InvokeModel with per-provider JSON","Converse API","SageMaker real-time endpoint","Bedrock batch inference"],"q":"You want one code path for chat across Claude, Llama, and Nova. Which API?","why":"The Converse API is the unified, model-agnostic chat interface across Bedrock providers."},{"answer":1,"options":["Fine-tuning","Continued pre-training","Distillation","Guardrails"],"q":"You have 10 GB of unlabeled medical journals and want the model to learn medical language. Which Bedrock customization?","why":"Continued pre-training adapts the base model on unlabeled domain text; fine-tuning needs labeled task pairs."},{"answer":1,"options":["Removing PII from training data","Training a smaller student model on a larger teacher's outputs","Compressing model weights with quantization","Filtering toxic content at inference"],"q":"What is model distillation?","why":"Distillation transfers the teacher's behavior into a cheaper, faster student model."},{"answer":2,"options":["Content filters","Denied topics","Automatic fine-tuning","Sensitive-information (PII) filters"],"q":"Which is NOT a Bedrock Guardrails capability?","why":"Guardrails filter and enforce policy on inputs/outputs; they never train or modify the model."},{"answer":1,"options":["A fine-tuned model","Managed RAG over your S3 data via Retrieve/RetrieveAndGenerate","Automatic prompt engineering","A private FM training cluster"],"q":"What does a Bedrock Knowledge Base give you without writing retrieval code?","why":"Knowledge Bases handle chunking, embeddings, and vector search; you just query."}],"resources":[{"label":"MLA-C02 exam guide (web)","note":"Scope authority for how deep the Bedrock/generative-AI content goes.","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-certification/latest/machine-learning-engineer-associate-02/machine-learning-engineer-associate-02.html"},{"label":"bli55 notes — Bedrock & agentic AI","note":"The freshest free community coverage of Bedrock, AgentCore, and agentic sections.","url":"https://github.com/bli55/notes-aws-machine-learning"},{"label":"Skill Builder — MLA-C02 prep","note":"Official prep plan with Bedrock and generative-AI modules.","url":"https://skillbuilder.aws"},{"label":"SageMaker & Bedrock docs","note":"Starting point in the AWS docs tree; navigate to Bedrock from here for API details.","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/"}],"title":"Bedrock & generative AI","week":3},{"blocks":[{"label":"Warm-up","steps":["Review your notes from Days 11–14 in one pass — 5 minutes per day, no deep re-reading.","Write down your three weakest topics so far. You'll check them against the practice exam results this afternoon."],"time":"0.5h"},{"label":"New material: agentic AI","steps":["Learn Bedrock Agents: an agent = FM + instructions + action groups + knowledge bases + guardrails. Action groups (Lambda functions or OpenAPI schemas) let the agent *do* things — query APIs, write tickets, run calculations — not just answer.","Learn the agent loop: reason → act (call a tool) → observe the result → repeat (ReAct-style). Multi-step tool-calling workflows emerge from this loop, chosen dynamically at runtime — that's what separates agents from fixed pipelines.","Learn agent memory: short-term memory (conversation history within a session) vs long-term memory (facts and preferences persisted across sessions).","Learn AgentCore: managed infrastructure for deploying and operating agents in production — runtime, memory, identity/gateway primitives, observability — so teams don't build the scaffolding themselves.","Decision rule to memorize: fixed, repeatable workflow → pipeline (Day 19). Dynamic task needing runtime tool choices → agent."],"time":"1.5h"},{"label":"Practice exam (timed) + miss review","steps":["Set up exam conditions: 85 questions, 170 minutes, no notes, no phone. Use the Tutorials Dojo MLA-C02 practice set or the Skill Builder official practice exam.","Take it in one sitting. Flag every question you're unsure about as you go — don't change answers on review pass unless you can name the reason.","Score it. Target: 65–70%+. Below that, the plan gains buffer weeks — that's the system working, not failing.","Review EVERY miss and every flagged guess: write the correct concept in one sentence and tag it with its domain (1–4) and study day.","Tally misses by domain. Your weakest domain becomes the priority for Week 4's remaining study time. Log the score and domain breakdown in the study tracker."],"time":"3h"}],"day":15,"domain":"Domain 2: ML Model and FM Development (24%)","isCheckpoint":true,"isReview":false,"keyTerms":[{"def":"FM + instructions + action groups + knowledge bases + guardrails for multi-step task completion.","term":"Bedrock Agents"},{"def":"Lambda/OpenAPI integrations that let a Bedrock Agent take real-world actions.","term":"Action groups"},{"def":"Reason → act → observe cycle by which agents work through multi-step tasks.","term":"ReAct loop"},{"def":"Short-term (in-session) vs long-term (cross-session) persisted context for agents.","term":"Agent memory"},{"def":"Managed production runtime infrastructure for deploying and operating AI agents.","term":"AgentCore"}],"notes":[{"heading":"Bedrock Agents","points":["An agent combines an FM with instructions, `action groups`, knowledge bases, and guardrails to complete multi-step tasks.","Action groups connect the agent to real systems via Lambda functions or OpenAPI schemas — e.g., 'check order status' calls your orders API.","The agent reasons about which action to take next, executes it, observes the result, and continues — the ReAct (reason + act) loop.","Knowledge bases give the agent your private data; guardrails keep its actions within policy. Same building blocks as Day 14, orchestrated."]},{"heading":"Agent memory","points":["`Short-term memory`: conversation history within a session — what was just said and done.","`Long-term memory`: facts, preferences, and learned context persisted across sessions.","Memory is what turns a single clever response into a useful assistant that remembers the user's context.","Exam framing: multi-turn personalization across sessions → long-term memory; within-conversation context → short-term."]},{"heading":"AgentCore","points":["AgentCore = managed infrastructure for running agents in production: deploy, scale, and operate agents without building the runtime scaffolding.","Covers the production concerns agents need: secure runtime, memory primitives, identity and gateway for tool access, observability.","Mental model: Bedrock Agents = the agent framework; AgentCore = the production runtime it (and other agents) run on.","This is new MLA-C02 territory — know it at the 'what problem does it solve' level, not API level."]},{"heading":"Agents vs pipelines","points":["Pipelines (Day 19): fixed, repeatable, auditable sequences — data prep → train → evaluate → deploy. Deterministic.","Agents: dynamic — the FM chooses tools and steps at runtime based on the task. Flexible but less predictable.","Rule: if you can draw the flowchart in advance, build a pipeline. If the steps depend on intermediate results, consider an agent.","In practice they compose: pipelines for the ML lifecycle, agents for the interactive/task layer on top."]},{"heading":"Practice exam protocol","points":["85 questions, 170 minutes — replicate it exactly. Stamina is part of the test.","65–70% is the go/no-go line for staying on the 6-week schedule; below it, add the buffer weeks from the plan.","Review every miss AND every lucky guess — a guess you got right is still a gap.","Tag each miss by domain; the domain with the most misses gets priority in Week 4.","Log the score in the study tracker. This number is your baseline for measuring Week 4–6 progress."]}],"objectives":["Explain how Bedrock Agents use action groups, knowledge bases, and memory to complete multi-step tasks.","Describe what AgentCore provides for running agents in production.","Complete a full timed practice exam, hit the 65–70% bar, and log every miss by domain."],"quiz":[{"answer":1,"options":["By fine-tuning on API documentation","Through action groups invoking Lambda functions or OpenAPI endpoints","Through Guardrails content filters","By writing directly to the vector store"],"q":"How does a Bedrock Agent interact with company systems (e.g., checking an order status)?","why":"Action groups are the agent's hands — Lambda/OpenAPI integrations it calls to take real actions."},{"answer":1,"options":["Short-term memory","Long-term memory","Checkpoint memory","Ephemeral context"],"q":"An agent should remember a user's preferences across sessions. Which memory type?","why":"Long-term memory persists facts across sessions; short-term covers only the current conversation."},{"answer":1,"options":["Training foundation models from scratch","Providing managed runtime infrastructure to deploy and operate agents in production","Replacing the Converse API","Storing training checkpoints"],"q":"What problem does AgentCore solve?","why":"AgentCore is the production runtime layer (deploy, scale, observe, identity) so teams don't build agent scaffolding themselves."}],"resources":[{"label":"bli55 notes — agentic AI / AgentCore","note":"Freshest free community notes on agents and AgentCore.","url":"https://github.com/bli55/notes-aws-machine-learning"},{"label":"Skill Builder — MLA-C02 prep","note":"Official prep; source for the official practice exam.","url":"https://skillbuilder.aws"},{"label":"MLA-C02 exam guide (PDF)","note":"Confirm agentic-AI scope in the Domain 2/3 task statements.","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/pdfs/aws-certification/latest/machine-learning-engineer-associate-02/machine-learning-engineer-associate-02.pdf"}],"title":"Checkpoint: agentic AI + practice exam","week":3},{"blocks":[{"label":"Warm-up","steps":["From memory, draw the four inference options as a 2x2: latency (ms vs minutes/hours) against traffic pattern (steady vs intermittent). Fill in which option fits each quadrant.","Re-read the Domain 3 task statements in the exam guide; mark every endpoint-related task."],"time":"0.5h"},{"label":"New material","steps":["Learn real-time inference: persistent HTTPS endpoint, low latency, production variants behind it; billed per instance-hour for as long as it exists — the #1 surprise-bill source.","Learn batch transform: offline scoring of S3 datasets, no persistent endpoint, mini-batches; most cost-effective for large scheduled scoring jobs.","Learn asynchronous inference: payloads up to 1 GB, processing up to 1 hour, results to S3 with SNS notification; for near-real-time workloads that don't need an immediate response.","Learn serverless inference: scales from zero, pay per invocation + compute duration; built-in cold starts; configure memory and concurrency; ideal for intermittent/spiky traffic.","Memorize the decision table: steady low-latency traffic → real-time; huge offline datasets → batch; big payloads/long processing → async; spiky intermittent → serverless.","Read the SageMaker inference docs and the bli55/ccsusan deployment notes."],"time":"2h"},{"code":"predictor = xgb.deploy(\n    initial_instance_count=1,\n    instance_type='ml.m5.large',\n    endpoint_name='day16-demo-endpoint',\n)\nprint(predictor.predict([[5.1, 3.5, 1.4, 0.2]]))\n\n# COST HYGIENE: endpoints bill per instance-hour while they exist.\n# Always delete when done:\npredictor.delete_endpoint()","codeLang":"python","label":"Hands-on lab","steps":["Deploy the XGBoost model from Day 11 as a real-time endpoint with the snippet below (ml.m5.large, one instance).","Send 5 test predictions and confirm responses. Note the endpoint status transitions (Creating → InService).","Check the billing angle: the endpoint bills per instance-hour from the moment it's InService — even with zero traffic.","DELETE the endpoint immediately with `predictor.delete_endpoint()`. Verify it's gone in the console. This step is the lab — an undeleted endpoint is a failed lab."],"time":"1.5h"},{"label":"Practice + teach-it-back","steps":["Do 20 practice questions on inference options (ihatesea69 repo).","Teach-it-back, out loud: 'I need to score 50 million records every night, and separately serve a fraud model at 100ms latency. Walk through both architectures and how each is billed.'"],"time":"1h"}],"day":16,"domain":"Domain 3: Deployment and Orchestration (24%)","isCheckpoint":false,"isReview":false,"keyTerms":[{"def":"Persistent low-latency HTTPS endpoint; billed per instance-hour while it exists.","term":"Real-time inference"},{"def":"Offline S3-to-S3 scoring job with no persistent endpoint; cheapest for large volumes.","term":"Batch transform"},{"def":"Up to 1 GB payloads / 1 hour processing; results to S3 with SNS notification.","term":"Asynchronous inference"},{"def":"Auto-scales from zero; pay per invocation; for intermittent or spiky traffic.","term":"Serverless inference"},{"def":"Weighted model versions behind one endpoint enabling traffic shifting.","term":"Production variants"},{"def":"How async inference tells you a result is ready in S3.","term":"SNS notification"}],"notes":[{"heading":"Real-time inference","points":["Persistent HTTPS endpoint backed by EC2 instances (production variants) — single-digit millisecond to second latency.","Billed per instance-hour for the entire lifetime of the endpoint, regardless of traffic. Idle endpoints still bill.","Supports production variants (traffic splitting), auto-scaling, and data capture — the full-featured option.","Use when: steady traffic needing immediate responses (fraud scoring, recommendations, chat)."]},{"heading":"Batch transform","points":["Offline scoring: point it at an S3 input prefix, it writes predictions to S3, then the job ends. No persistent endpoint.","Mini-batch handling and input/output filtering built in; most cost-effective for large volumes.","Use when: scheduled scoring of large datasets where latency doesn't matter (nightly churn scoring, bulk personalization).","Exam signal: 'millions of records' + 'no real-time requirement' → batch transform, every time."]},{"heading":"Asynchronous inference","points":["For payloads up to 1 GB and processing up to 1 hour — beyond real-time limits.","Request returns immediately; results land in S3 and an SNS notification fires on completion.","Near-real-time use cases: large document/image processing, long-running feature extraction.","Exam signal: 'large payload' or 'takes 20 minutes per request' → async inference."]},{"heading":"Serverless inference","points":["Scales from zero to peak automatically; you pay per invocation plus compute duration — no idle billing.","Cold starts on scale-from-zero; configure memory size and max concurrency to bound cost and latency.","Use when: intermittent or spiky traffic where a persistent endpoint would idle (internal tools, bursty APIs).","Exam signal: 'unpredictable traffic' + 'minimize cost' → serverless inference."]},{"heading":"The decision table (memorize)","points":["Steady traffic + immediate response → `real-time endpoint`.","Huge dataset + no latency need → `batch transform`.","Big payload (up to 1 GB) / long processing (up to 1 h) → `asynchronous inference`.","Spiky/intermittent + cost-sensitive → `serverless inference`.","When two options seem plausible, re-read the scenario for payload size, latency, and traffic pattern — one of them always disqualifies an option."]},{"heading":"Cost hygiene (non-negotiable)","points":["Real-time endpoints are the classic surprise bill: they charge per instance-hour from creation to deletion.","Lab rule: every endpoint you create gets deleted in the same session. Verify deletion in the console.","Your $5 billing alarm is the backstop, not the plan — the plan is deleting what you create.","Batch transform and async jobs end themselves; only persistent endpoints (real-time) and provisioned Bedrock throughput need manual teardown."]}],"objectives":["Compare real-time, batch transform, asynchronous, and serverless inference and pick the right one for a scenario.","Deploy a real-time endpoint and then delete it, practicing cost hygiene.","Explain how each option is billed and where the cost traps are."],"quiz":[{"answer":1,"options":["Real-time endpoint","Batch transform","Asynchronous inference","Serverless inference"],"q":"You must score 10 million records in S3 overnight. No latency requirement. Best option?","why":"Batch transform is built for offline S3-to-S3 scoring with no persistent endpoint cost."},{"answer":2,"options":["Real-time endpoint","Batch transform","Asynchronous inference","Serverless inference"],"q":"Each request has an 800 MB payload and takes 20 minutes to process. Best option?","why":"Async inference handles up to 1 GB payloads and 1-hour processing with S3 output + SNS notification."},{"answer":2,"options":["Real-time endpoint with 4 instances","Batch transform","Serverless inference","Asynchronous inference"],"q":"Traffic is spiky and unpredictable; the API idles for hours. Cheapest fit?","why":"Serverless scales to zero and bills per invocation — no idle instance-hours."},{"answer":1,"options":["Number of predictions served","Per instance-hour for as long as the endpoint exists","Size of the model artifact","Number of production variants"],"q":"What primarily drives the cost of a real-time endpoint?","why":"Real-time endpoints bill per instance-hour from creation to deletion, regardless of traffic."},{"answer":2,"options":["Batch transform","Serverless inference","Real-time inference","Asynchronous inference"],"q":"Steady high traffic needing 100 ms responses for fraud scoring. Best option?","why":"Steady low-latency traffic is the textbook real-time endpoint case."}],"resources":[{"label":"SageMaker docs — inference options","note":"Authoritative comparison of real-time, batch, async, and serverless inference.","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/"},{"label":"bli55 notes — deployment","note":"Community notes on endpoint types and selection.","url":"https://github.com/bli55/notes-aws-machine-learning"},{"label":"ccsusan notes — MLOps & deployment","note":"Cheat-sheet style endpoint comparison tables.","url":"https://github.com/ccsusan/aws-mla-certification-notes"},{"label":"ihatesea69 — hands-on labs","note":"Deploy-and-delete lab walkthroughs.","url":"https://github.com/ihatesea69/aws-mla-study-notes-and-hands-on-labs"}],"title":"Inference endpoints","week":4},{"blocks":[{"label":"Warm-up","steps":["From memory: yesterday's decision table — write all four inference options and one scenario each. Check against Day 16 notes.","Write one sentence each for blue/green, canary, and A/B from general software knowledge — today you learn the SageMaker specifics."],"time":"0.5h"},{"label":"New material","steps":["Learn production variants: multiple model versions behind one endpoint with traffic weights — the mechanism all three rollout strategies use.","Learn blue/green: deploy the new model as a second variant, shift 100% traffic at once, instant rollback by shifting back. Safest for fast rollback.","Learn canary: route a small percentage (e.g., 5%) to the new variant, monitor metrics, ramp gradually. Safest for catching problems early.","Learn A/B testing: split traffic to compare model versions on live business metrics — the goal is measurement, not safe rollout.","Learn auto-scaling: target-tracking scaling policies on `InvocationsPerInstance` (or a custom CloudWatch metric); set min/max/desired instance counts.","Learn multi-model endpoints: many models on one endpoint fleet, loaded from S3 into memory on demand — the cost answer for many low-traffic models.","Learn inference pipelines: a linear sequence of containers (preprocessing → model → postprocessing) invoked as one endpoint; vs multi-container direct invocation.","Read the SageMaker deployment docs and bli55/ccsusan deployment notes."],"time":"2h"},{"label":"Hands-on lab","steps":["On paper (or in the console if budget allows): design a canary deployment for the Day 16 XGBoost endpoint — two production variants (v1 weight 95, v2 weight 5), the CloudWatch metrics you'd watch, and the ramp criteria.","Add an auto-scaling policy to the design: target-tracking on InvocationsPerInstance, min 1 / max 4 instances. Write down what happens during a 10x traffic spike.","Sketch a multi-model endpoint alternative: 20 small models, one endpoint — when does this beat 20 real-time endpoints? (Answer: always on cost; check the memory/latency tradeoff.)","Tear down anything you created. Verify no endpoints remain in the console."],"time":"1.5h"},{"label":"Practice + teach-it-back","steps":["Do 20 practice questions on deployment strategies (ihatesea69 repo).","Teach-it-back, out loud: 'My new model might be broken in a way offline metrics can't catch. I need to validate on live traffic with instant rollback. Blue/green or canary — and why?'"],"time":"1h"}],"day":17,"domain":"Domain 3: Deployment and Orchestration (24%)","isCheckpoint":false,"isReview":false,"keyTerms":[{"def":"Weighted model versions behind one endpoint; the mechanism for traffic shifting.","term":"Production variants"},{"def":"Full traffic flip to the new version with instant rollback by flipping back.","term":"Blue/green deployment"},{"def":"Small traffic slice to the new version first, monitored, then gradually ramped.","term":"Canary deployment"},{"def":"Traffic split to compare model versions on live business metrics.","term":"A/B testing"},{"def":"Auto-scaling policy holding a metric (e.g., InvocationsPerInstance) at a target value.","term":"Target-tracking scaling"},{"def":"One endpoint fleet serving many models loaded from S3 on demand; cost-efficient for low-traffic models.","term":"Multi-model endpoint"},{"def":"Linear container sequence (preprocess → predict → postprocess) behind a single endpoint.","term":"Inference pipeline"}],"notes":[{"heading":"Production variants: the underlying mechanism","points":["One endpoint can host multiple `production variants` — model versions with traffic weights (e.g., v1: 90, v2: 10).","All rollout strategies are just different ways of setting and moving these weights.","Variants can also use different instance types — useful for cost/performance comparison.","Exam framing: any 'shift traffic between model versions' scenario starts with production variants."]},{"heading":"Blue/green vs canary vs A/B","points":["`Blue/green`: new version deployed alongside old; traffic flips 100% at once. Rollback = flip back. Best when you need instant, certain rollback.","`Canary`: small slice of traffic (5%) goes to the new version first; monitor, then ramp. Best when you want to catch live issues with minimal blast radius.","`A/B testing`: traffic split to *compare* versions on business metrics. Goal is measurement (which model is better?), not safety.","Exam discriminator: 'instant rollback' → blue/green. 'Gradual validation on live traffic' → canary. 'Compare performance' → A/B."]},{"heading":"Auto-scaling","points":["Target-tracking scaling policies adjust instance count to hold a metric at a target — typically `InvocationsPerInstance`.","You set min, max, and desired capacity; SageMaker adds/removes instances as traffic moves.","Custom CloudWatch metrics (e.g., model latency, queue depth) work when invocations-per-instance doesn't capture the constraint.","Scale-in should be conservative (cooldowns) to avoid flapping; the exam occasionally tests this operational detail."]},{"heading":"Multi-model endpoints","points":["One endpoint fleet serves many models; models are loaded from S3 into memory on demand and unloaded when idle.","The cost answer for dozens or hundreds of low-traffic models — one fleet instead of one endpoint per model.","Tradeoff: first request for an unloaded model pays a load-from-S3 latency penalty; memory limits how many stay resident.","Exam signal: 'many models, each rarely invoked, minimize cost' → multi-model endpoint."]},{"heading":"Inference pipelines","points":["An `inference pipeline` chains containers in sequence behind one endpoint: preprocessing → model → postprocessing, invoked as a single call.","Alternative: multi-container endpoint with direct invocation, where the client picks which container to hit.","Use pipelines when every request needs the same transform → predict → format flow; it keeps client code simple.","Up to 15 containers can be chained — know it's a sequence, not a parallel fan-out."]}],"objectives":["Distinguish blue/green, canary, and A/B deployments and choose the right one for a rollout scenario.","Explain SageMaker auto-scaling and which metrics drive it.","Describe multi-model endpoints and inference pipelines and when each saves money or complexity."],"quiz":[{"answer":1,"options":["Blue/green","Canary","A/B testing","Shadow deployment"],"q":"You want to validate a new model on 5% of live traffic, monitor it, then ramp gradually. Which strategy?","why":"Canary = small live slice first, monitored, then ramped; built for minimal blast radius."},{"answer":2,"options":["Canary","A/B testing","Blue/green","Multi-model endpoint"],"q":"You need the ability to roll back to the previous model instantly if something breaks. Which strategy?","why":"Blue/green keeps both versions live; rollback is an instant traffic-weight flip."},{"answer":1,"options":["CPUUtilization only","InvocationsPerInstance","Model artifact size","Number of variants"],"q":"Which metric does SageMaker target-tracking auto-scaling most commonly use?","why":"InvocationsPerInstance is the standard target-tracking metric for endpoint scaling."},{"answer":1,"options":["50 real-time endpoints","One multi-model endpoint","50 batch transform jobs per day","Serverless inference per model"],"q":"You have 50 small models, each invoked a few times per day. Cheapest serving option?","why":"A multi-model endpoint shares one fleet across all models, loading each from S3 on demand."},{"answer":1,"options":["Instant rollback capability","Comparing model versions on live business metrics","Reducing inference latency","Cutting endpoint cost"],"q":"What is the goal of A/B testing a model deployment?","why":"A/B testing measures which version performs better live; safety-focused rollouts are blue/green or canary."}],"resources":[{"label":"SageMaker docs — deployment & variants","note":"Authoritative detail on production variants, auto-scaling, and multi-model endpoints.","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/"},{"label":"bli55 notes — deployment","note":"Community notes on rollout strategies and scaling.","url":"https://github.com/bli55/notes-aws-machine-learning"},{"label":"ccsusan notes — MLOps & deployment","note":"Cheat-sheet comparisons of blue/green vs canary vs A/B.","url":"https://github.com/ccsusan/aws-mla-certification-notes"}],"title":"Deployment strategies","week":4},{"blocks":[{"label":"Warm-up","steps":["From memory: sketch the path a model takes from training to production (train → ? → ? → endpoint). Leave blanks where you're unsure — today fills them.","Write one paragraph on why regulated industries can't just deploy whatever a data scientist trained."],"time":"0.5h"},{"label":"New material","steps":["Learn the Model Registry: a versioned catalog of models (model groups → versions). Each version carries metrics, artifacts, and lineage. Approval workflow: `PendingManualApproval` → `Approved` or `Rejected`. Pipelines gate deployment on approval — unapproved models don't ship.","Learn Model Cards: standardized documentation for a model — intended uses, training data, evaluation results, limitations, ethical considerations. Written for stakeholders, auditors, and future-you, not just engineers.","Learn lineage tracking: every model version links back to its training job, dataset, and code — the audit trail that answers 'how was this model built?'","Learn JumpStart: prebuilt foundation and classical models plus solution templates. Deploy with one click, or fine-tune open models on your data. The fastest path from zero to a working endpoint.","Connect the dots: train → register version (registry) → document (model card) → approve → deploy (endpoint or JumpStart). Read the SageMaker docs sections on each."],"time":"2h"},{"label":"Hands-on lab","steps":["In SageMaker Studio: register your Day 11 XGBoost model in the Model Registry (create a model group, register a version with its metrics).","Create a Model Card for it: intended use, training data description, evaluation metrics from Day 13, limitations. Export/share it from the console.","Open JumpStart in Studio: browse the model catalog, pick a small open model, and walk through the deploy flow — stop before launching (or deploy then immediately delete the endpoint).","Verify in the registry that your version shows PendingManualApproval; approve it. Then confirm no endpoints are left running."],"time":"1.5h"},{"label":"Practice + teach-it-back","steps":["Do 20 practice questions on registry/cards/JumpStart (ihatesea69 repo).","Teach-it-back, out loud: 'Our auditor asks: which data trained the model currently in production, and who approved it? Walk me through exactly where I'd find each answer.'"],"time":"1h"}],"day":18,"domain":"Domain 3: Deployment and Orchestration (24%)","isCheckpoint":false,"isReview":false,"keyTerms":[{"def":"Versioned catalog of models with metrics, lineage, and an approval workflow.","term":"Model Registry"},{"def":"Default registry status; a human must approve the version before it deploys.","term":"PendingManualApproval"},{"def":"Standardized documentation of a model's intended use, data, evaluation, limitations, and ethics.","term":"Model Card"},{"def":"Automatic record linking model versions to training jobs, datasets, and code.","term":"Lineage tracking"},{"def":"SageMaker catalog of prebuilt models and solution templates; deploy or fine-tune quickly.","term":"JumpStart"}],"notes":[{"heading":"Model Registry","points":["A versioned catalog: `model groups` contain `model versions`, each with artifacts, metrics, and lineage.","Approval workflow: versions start at `PendingManualApproval`; a human moves them to `Approved` or `Rejected`.","Deployment pipelines gate on approval — the standard pattern is 'only Approved versions reach production endpoints'.","This is the MLOps answer to governance: no more 'which model is in prod?' — the registry is the source of truth."]},{"heading":"Model Cards","points":["Standardized model documentation: intended uses and out-of-scope uses, training data summary, evaluation results, limitations, ethical considerations.","Audience: stakeholders, auditors, compliance, and future team members — not just the training engineer.","Exam angle: 'document a model's limitations and intended use for review' → Model Cards, not the registry (which tracks versions/approvals).","Cards can be versioned alongside the model and exported/shared for review workflows."]},{"heading":"Lineage tracking","points":["SageMaker tracks lineage automatically: model version → training job → dataset + code + hyperparameters.","Answers the audit question: 'exactly what data and code produced the model in production?'","Lineage flows both directions: from a production endpoint back to its training data, or from a dataset forward to every model trained on it.","Pairs with the registry: versions tell you *what*, lineage tells you *how*."]},{"heading":"SageMaker JumpStart","points":["Catalog of prebuilt models (foundation models and classical ML) plus end-to-end solution templates.","Two paths: deploy as-is with one click, or fine-tune an open model on your own data first.","Fastest path from zero to working endpoint — the exam's answer for 'get a baseline model running quickly'.","Deployed JumpStart models are normal SageMaker endpoints afterward: same variants, scaling, and (importantly) same per-hour billing — delete when done."]},{"heading":"The full lifecycle (memorize the order)","points":["`Train` → `Register` version in the registry → `Document` with a Model Card → `Approve` (human) → `Deploy` to an endpoint.","Lineage is recorded automatically at each step; the registry is the gate between experimentation and production.","Tomorrow's pipelines automate exactly this sequence — today you did it by hand so the automation makes sense."]}],"objectives":["Explain the Model Registry approval workflow and why pipelines gate on it.","Describe what a Model Card documents and who it's for.","Use JumpStart to deploy or fine-tune a prebuilt model."],"quiz":[{"answer":1,"options":["Approved","PendingManualApproval","Rejected","Archived"],"q":"A model version must be human-approved before any pipeline deploys it. Which registry status enforces this?","why":"Versions start at PendingManualApproval; pipelines gate deployment until a human moves them to Approved."},{"answer":1,"options":["A training job log","A Model Card","A checkpoint file","An endpoint configuration"],"q":"An auditor needs the model's intended uses, limitations, and evaluation results in one document. What do you produce?","why":"Model Cards are the standardized documentation artifact for intended use, evaluation, limitations, and ethical considerations."},{"answer":1,"options":["Train a custom model from scratch","Deploy a prebuilt model via JumpStart","Write a Lambda from scratch","Run batch transform manually"],"q":"You need a working text-classification endpoint by end of day as a baseline. Fastest path?","why":"JumpStart's prebuilt models deploy with one click — the exam's 'get running quickly' answer."},{"answer":1,"options":["Model Card","Lineage tracking","JumpStart","Inference pipeline"],"q":"'Which dataset and code produced the model currently in production?' — what answers this?","why":"Lineage links each model version back to its training job, dataset, and code — the audit trail."},{"answer":1,"options":["Between train and register","Between approve and deploy","Between document and approve","No gate is needed"],"q":"Where does the deployment gate belong in the lifecycle: train → register → document → approve → deploy?","why":"Only Approved registry versions reach production; the gate sits between approval and deployment."}],"resources":[{"label":"SageMaker docs — registry, cards, JumpStart","note":"Authoritative reference for the registry workflow, Model Cards, and JumpStart.","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/"},{"label":"bli55 notes — MLOps","note":"Community notes on registry and governance topics.","url":"https://github.com/bli55/notes-aws-machine-learning"},{"label":"ccsusan notes — MLOps & deployment","note":"Cheat-sheet summaries of registry and JumpStart.","url":"https://github.com/ccsusan/aws-mla-certification-notes"}],"title":"Model Registry, Model Cards & JumpStart","week":4},{"blocks":[{"label":"Warm-up","steps":["From memory, rewrite yesterday's lifecycle (train → register → document → approve → deploy) as a flowchart with decision diamonds. Where would automation help most?","Write down everything you've done manually so far that a machine should do: retraining, evaluation gating, registration, deployment."],"time":"0.5h"},{"label":"New material","steps":["Learn SageMaker Pipelines: ML-native workflow orchestration. Step types: Processing (data prep), Training, Evaluation, RegisterModel (to the registry), Condition (gate on a metric, e.g., accuracy ≥ threshold), Deploy/CreateEndpoint. Pipeline definitions are versioned; every execution is tracked.","Learn the Condition step deeply: it's how 'only deploy if the model is good enough' is implemented — the exam tests this pattern directly.","Learn Step Functions: general-purpose serverless orchestration across AWS services, with SageMaker service integrations. Use it when the workflow spans non-ML steps (e.g., notify Slack, update DynamoDB, call Lambda) — Pipelines is ML-native, Step Functions is general.","Learn EventBridge: cron/rate scheduling ('retrain every Sunday at 2am') and event-driven triggers (new data lands in S3 → start the pipeline).","Learn retraining triggers: scheduled, new-data-arrived, data drift detected, performance decay. Memorize the list — it's a classic exam question.","Read the SageMaker Pipelines docs and the bli55/ccsusan orchestration notes."],"time":"2h"},{"label":"Hands-on lab","steps":["On paper first: sketch a minimal pipeline for the XGBoost model — ProcessingStep (prepare data) → TrainingStep → EvaluationStep (compute F1) → ConditionStep (F1 ≥ 0.85) → RegisterModelStep → DeployStep. Label what each step consumes and produces.","In SageMaker Studio (or as a written spec if you prefer zero cost): build the pipeline definition with the SDK's workflow steps. Keep it minimal — correctness of the DAG matters more than fancy processing.","Add an EventBridge rule sketch: cron schedule for weekly retraining. Write the cron expression and which pipeline it targets.","If you executed anything, verify no pipeline executions are left running and no endpoints were created (or delete them)."],"time":"1.5h"},{"label":"Practice + teach-it-back","steps":["Do 20 practice questions on orchestration (ihatesea69 repo).","Teach-it-back, out loud: 'New training data lands in S3 every night. I want the model retrained, evaluated, and only deployed if it beats the current production model. Draw the architecture and name every AWS piece.'"],"time":"1h"}],"day":19,"domain":"Domain 3: Deployment and Orchestration (24%)","isCheckpoint":false,"isReview":false,"keyTerms":[{"def":"ML-native workflow orchestration with Processing/Training/Evaluation/Register/Condition/Deploy steps.","term":"SageMaker Pipelines"},{"def":"Pipeline gate that proceeds only if a metric meets a threshold (e.g., F1 >= 0.85).","term":"Condition step"},{"def":"General serverless orchestration across AWS services; use when workflows span non-ML steps.","term":"Step Functions"},{"def":"Cron scheduling and event-driven triggers that start pipelines and state machines.","term":"EventBridge"},{"def":"Pipeline step that writes the trained model to the Model Registry.","term":"RegisterModel step"},{"def":"Scheduled, new-data, data-drift, or performance-decay events that kick off retraining.","term":"Retraining triggers"}],"notes":[{"heading":"SageMaker Pipelines","points":["ML-native orchestration: purpose-built steps — `Processing`, `Training`, `Evaluation`, `RegisterModel`, `Condition`, `CreateEndpoint/Deploy`.","Pipeline definitions are versioned and every execution is tracked — reproducibility is built in, not bolted on.","`RegisterModel` writes to the Model Registry (Day 18); the pipeline and registry are designed to work together.","This is the automation of yesterday's manual lifecycle: the DAG *is* the MLOps process."]},{"heading":"The Condition step","points":["Gates the DAG on a metric: e.g., proceed to RegisterModel only if `F1 >= 0.85`.","This is how 'never deploy a worse model' is implemented — the single most exam-tested pipeline pattern.","Failed conditions can route to a notification step instead of silently stopping — pair with SNS for visibility.","Evaluation step writes metrics to a JSON file in S3; the Condition step reads a value via JsonGet."]},{"heading":"Step Functions vs Pipelines","points":["`SageMaker Pipelines`: ML-native — training/evaluation/registry steps, experiment tracking integration. Use when the workflow is an ML lifecycle.","`Step Functions`: general serverless orchestration across all AWS services, with optimized SageMaker integrations. Use when the workflow includes non-ML steps (Lambda, DynamoDB, notifications, human approvals).","They compose: a Step Functions state machine can invoke a SageMaker Pipeline as one of its steps.","Exam discriminator: 'ML workflow with registry integration' → Pipelines. 'Workflow spanning many AWS services' → Step Functions."]},{"heading":"EventBridge scheduling and triggers","points":["`Cron/rate rules`: 'retrain every Sunday 02:00' — the standard scheduled-retraining trigger.","`Event-driven rules`: S3 object-created → start pipeline; registry approval → deploy. Reactive instead of scheduled.","EventBridge is the scheduler; it doesn't do the work — it starts the pipeline or state machine that does.","Exam signal: any 'automatically retrain on a schedule / when data arrives' scenario names EventBridge."]},{"heading":"Retraining triggers (memorize the list)","points":["`Scheduled`: fixed cadence (weekly/monthly) — simplest, most common exam answer.","`New data arrived`: S3 event triggers the pipeline — fresh data, fresh model.","`Data drift detected`: SageMaker Model Monitor (Domain 4) flags drift → retrain.","`Performance decay`: live metrics (accuracy, business KPI) drop below threshold → retrain.","Real systems combine them: scheduled baseline + event-driven overrides."]}],"objectives":["Design a SageMaker Pipeline (prep → train → evaluate → condition → register → deploy) and explain each step type.","Choose between SageMaker Pipelines and Step Functions for a given workflow.","Schedule a pipeline with EventBridge and explain retraining triggers."],"quiz":[{"answer":1,"options":["A TrainingStep with max_runtime","A Condition step gating on the evaluation metric","An EventBridge cron rule","A second pipeline"],"q":"How do you implement 'only deploy the model if F1 is at least 0.85' in a SageMaker Pipeline?","why":"The Condition step branches the DAG on metric thresholds — the standard deploy-gate pattern."},{"answer":1,"options":["SageMaker Pipelines alone","Step Functions","EventBridge alone","Batch transform"],"q":"Your workflow retrains a model, then updates DynamoDB, notifies Slack, and needs a human approval. Best orchestrator?","why":"Non-ML steps across many services is the Step Functions case; Pipelines is ML-native."},{"answer":1,"options":["SageMaker Training","EventBridge","CloudWatch Logs","SNS"],"q":"You want the pipeline to run every Sunday at 2 AM. Which service schedules it?","why":"EventBridge cron/rate rules are the scheduler that starts pipelines on a cadence."},{"answer":3,"options":["Scheduled cadence","New data arriving in S3","Data drift detected","Changing the IAM role"],"q":"Which is NOT a standard retraining trigger?","why":"Retraining is triggered by schedule, new data, drift, or performance decay — not IAM changes."},{"answer":1,"options":["They cannot be combined","The state machine invokes a SageMaker Pipeline as one of its steps","Pipelines can only run standalone","Rewrite the pipeline as Lambda functions"],"q":"A Step Functions state machine needs a model-training stage with registry integration. How do they compose?","why":"Step Functions orchestrates broadly and can call a SageMaker Pipeline for the ML-native stages."}],"resources":[{"label":"SageMaker docs — Pipelines","note":"Authoritative reference for pipeline step types and the Condition pattern.","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/"},{"label":"bli55 notes — MLOps / orchestration","note":"Community notes on Pipelines, Step Functions, and EventBridge.","url":"https://github.com/bli55/notes-aws-machine-learning"},{"label":"ccsusan notes — MLOps & deployment","note":"Cheat-sheet summaries of orchestration options.","url":"https://github.com/ccsusan/aws-mla-certification-notes"}],"title":"Orchestration: Pipelines, Step Functions, EventBridge","week":4},{"blocks":[{"label":"Review: endpoints (Day 16)","steps":["Closed-note: rewrite the inference decision table (real-time / batch / async / serverless) with one scenario each. Check against Day 16 notes; fix gaps.","Recite the billing model of each option out loud. Where is the cost trap in each?","Confirm in the AWS console: zero endpoints running, zero training jobs running. Cost hygiene is part of the review."],"time":"1h"},{"label":"Review: deployment strategies (Day 17)","steps":["Closed-note: define blue/green, canary, and A/B in one sentence each, then state the exam discriminator for each.","Sketch a canary deployment with production variants and weights from memory; add the auto-scaling policy (metric, min/max).","Explain multi-model endpoints and inference pipelines to an imaginary interviewer in under 2 minutes each."],"time":"1h"},{"label":"Review: registry & JumpStart (Day 18)","steps":["Closed-note: write the lifecycle order (train → register → document → approve → deploy) and what artifact each step produces.","One sentence each: what the registry tracks, what a Model Card documents, what lineage answers, what JumpStart is for.","List the registry approval statuses and which one gates production deployment."],"time":"1h"},{"label":"Review: orchestration (Day 19)","steps":["Closed-note: draw the minimal pipeline DAG (processing → training → evaluation → condition → register → deploy) with step types labeled.","State the Pipelines-vs-Step-Functions decision rule and the four retraining triggers from memory.","Write an EventBridge cron use case and an event-driven use case, one line each."],"time":"1h"},{"label":"Cumulative quiz + teach-it-back","steps":["Take the 10-question cumulative quiz below closed-note. Target: 8+/10.","For every miss: write the correct concept in one sentence, tag it with its day (16–19), and add it to the Week 5 hit list.","Teach-it-back finale, out loud: 'Walk me through the entire journey of a model from raw S3 data to a canary-deployed endpoint with weekly retraining.' If you stall anywhere, that's tomorrow's warm-up."],"time":"1h"}],"day":20,"domain":"Domain 3: Deployment and Orchestration (24%)","isCheckpoint":false,"isReview":true,"keyTerms":[{"def":"The four inference options; chosen by latency, payload, and traffic pattern.","term":"Real-time / batch / async / serverless"},{"def":"Rollout strategies: instant flip, gradual validation, live comparison.","term":"Blue/green / canary / A/B"},{"def":"Weighted model versions behind one endpoint enabling traffic shifting.","term":"Production variants"},{"def":"Versioned model catalog with approval workflow gating production.","term":"Model Registry"},{"def":"Standardized documentation: intended use, data, evaluation, limitations, ethics.","term":"Model Card"},{"def":"Pipeline gate on a metric threshold before registration/deployment.","term":"Condition step"},{"def":"Cron scheduling and event-driven triggers for pipelines.","term":"EventBridge"},{"def":"Schedule, new data, drift, or performance decay.","term":"Retraining triggers"}],"notes":[{"heading":"Day 16 recap: inference options","points":["`Real-time`: persistent endpoint, per instance-hour billing, steady low-latency traffic.","`Batch transform`: offline S3-to-S3 scoring, no persistent endpoint, cheapest at volume.","`Asynchronous`: up to 1 GB / 1 hour, S3 output + SNS notification.","`Serverless`: scales to zero, pay per invocation, intermittent/spiky traffic."]},{"heading":"Day 17 recap: rollouts and scaling","points":["`Blue/green`: instant full flip, instant rollback. `Canary`: gradual live validation. `A/B`: live comparison.","All three ride on `production variants` with traffic weights.","`Target-tracking` auto-scaling on `InvocationsPerInstance`; set min/max.","`Multi-model endpoints` share one fleet across many low-traffic models; `inference pipelines` chain preprocess → predict → postprocess."]},{"heading":"Day 18 recap: governance","points":["Lifecycle: `train → register → document (Model Card) → approve → deploy`.","Registry: versions + `PendingManualApproval` → `Approved`/`Rejected`; the deployment gate.","`Lineage`: model → training job → dataset + code (the audit trail).","`JumpStart`: prebuilt models/solutions; deploy or fine-tune fast."]},{"heading":"Day 19 recap: orchestration","points":["Pipeline DAG: `Processing → Training → Evaluation → Condition → RegisterModel → Deploy`.","`Condition` step = the deploy gate (metric threshold).","`Pipelines` for ML-native workflows; `Step Functions` when non-ML services join; `EventBridge` schedules or event-triggers.","Retraining triggers: scheduled, new data, data drift, performance decay."]}],"objectives":["Recall the four inference options and their decision rules without notes.","Reconstruct the rollout strategies, registry lifecycle, and pipeline DAG from memory.","Score 8+/10 on the cumulative quiz; log any remaining gaps for Week 5."],"quiz":[{"answer":2,"options":["Real-time endpoint","Serverless inference","Batch transform","Asynchronous inference"],"q":"Nightly scoring of 50M S3 records, no latency need. Which option?","why":"Batch transform = offline S3-to-S3 scoring with no persistent endpoint."},{"answer":2,"options":["Blue/green","A/B testing","Canary","Multi-model endpoint"],"q":"5% of live traffic to the new model first, then ramp. Which strategy?","why":"Canary validates on a small live slice before gradual rollout."},{"answer":1,"options":["30 real-time endpoints","Multi-model endpoint","30 serverless endpoints","Batch transform"],"q":"30 rarely-used models, minimize serving cost. Which option?","why":"One multi-model endpoint fleet shares cost across many low-traffic models."},{"answer":1,"options":["Approved","PendingManualApproval","Rejected","Draft"],"q":"Which registry status must a version leave before a gated pipeline deploys it?","why":"Versions start PendingManualApproval; only Approved versions pass the deployment gate."},{"answer":1,"options":["TrainingStep","Condition step","RegisterModel","ProcessingStep"],"q":"'Only deploy if the new model beats production F1' — which pipeline step implements this?","why":"The Condition step branches the DAG on metric thresholds."},{"answer":1,"options":["SageMaker Pipelines alone","Step Functions","EventBridge alone","JumpStart"],"q":"Workflow includes retraining, DynamoDB updates, and Slack alerts. Best orchestrator?","why":"Non-ML steps across services = Step Functions; Pipelines stays ML-native."},{"answer":2,"options":["Real-time endpoint","Batch transform","Asynchronous inference","Serverless inference"],"q":"800 MB payload, 25-minute processing, result needed later. Which option?","why":"Async inference covers up to 1 GB payloads and 1-hour processing with S3 + SNS delivery."},{"answer":1,"options":["Training logs","Model Card","Endpoint config","Pipeline definition"],"q":"Which document lists a model's intended uses, limitations, and ethical considerations?","why":"Model Cards are the standardized stakeholder/auditor documentation artifact."},{"answer":1,"options":["SNS","EventBridge","CloudWatch Logs","SQS"],"q":"Retrain every Monday 3 AM. Which service triggers the pipeline?","why":"EventBridge cron rules schedule pipeline executions."},{"answer":1,"options":["Multi-model endpoint","Inference pipeline","Canary deployment","Batch transform"],"q":"Preprocessing → prediction → postprocessing as one endpoint call. What is this?","why":"An inference pipeline chains containers sequentially behind a single endpoint."}],"resources":[{"label":"SageMaker docs","note":"Re-check any topic you missed on the quiz — inference, deployment, pipelines.","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/sagemaker/"},{"label":"MLA-C02 exam guide (PDF)","note":"Re-read the Domain 3 task statements; confirm full coverage.","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/pdfs/aws-certification/latest/machine-learning-engineer-associate-02/machine-learning-engineer-associate-02.pdf"},{"label":"Skill Builder — MLA-C02 prep","note":"Official practice questions for any weak Domain 3 area.","url":"https://skillbuilder.aws"}],"title":"Review: deployment & orchestration","week":4});